--TEST--
Core\Ldap::connect asks `ldap.connect` about the block's name before it reads any configuration, so an ungranted name throws `RuntimeError` and learns nothing about which blocks exist — `rule:security/ldap-pool-is-bound-as-its-block` and `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `ldap.connect` grants `corp` and nothing else. Both names below are blocks
// in `nvs.toml`: the grant is what differs, so the throw is about the grant.
try {
    var $staff = Core\Ldap::connect("staff");
    echo "opened staff", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// A name that is no block at all throws the same message, so a program
// without the grant cannot tell which blocks exist.
try {
    var $nothing = Core\Ldap::connect("nothing");
    echo "opened nothing", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// A denial is a runtime error, and the program keeps running.
echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.ldap]
connect = ["corp"]

[ldap.corp]
url = "ldaps://127.0.0.1:1"

[ldap.staff]
url = "ldaps://127.0.0.1:1"
--EXPECT--
Core\Ldap::connect needs the capability `ldap.connect` for staff, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.ldap]`
Core\Ldap::connect needs the capability `ldap.connect` for nothing, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.ldap]`
still running
